<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 37.5%;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in Our World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 37.5%;"><b>Theme:</b> It's an Orange Cat.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify pictures that show what happened in a simple story heard. Reading: Identify simple words for very common symbols. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can listen to a simple story and identify corresponding pictures that illustrate key events (e.g., recognizing the picture of a blue bird mentioned in the story). Can identify simple words for very common symbols, matching them to images (e.g., linking "flower" with its picture). Can identify the initial sounds of letters of the alphabet in common objects and things (e.g., /f/ in "fish," /b/ in "bird," and /k/ in "cat").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts a "Color Scavenger Hunt" using flashcards from Lesson 1. The teacher shows a color flashcard (e.g., "blue") and asks, "What color is it?" Students respond chorally "Blue!" Then the teacher shows an object flashcard (e.g., a "bird") and asks, "What is it?" Students respond "Bird!" The teacher then says, "Find something blue!" or "Show me a blue bird!" using previously introduced flashcards (e.g., a blue bird, a green tree, a red flower, an orange cat, a purple house, a green leaf, the blue sky). This activates prior vocabulary and connects to the theme "It's an Orange Cat" and "Colors in Our World."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of an orange cat. The teacher points to the cat and says, "It is an orange cat." The teacher clearly pronounces the initial sound /k/ in "cat" while pointing to the word 'cat' written below the picture. The teacher repeats this with a blue bird (/b/ in 'bird') and a red flower (/f/ in 'flower'), emphasizing the initial sounds and the /l/ sound in "blue" and "leaf" as they appear. The teacher models matching the picture to the simple word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple yes/no questions and pointing prompts.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up an orange cat flashcard) "Is this an orange cat? Yes or no?" (Students nod/shake head or say "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a blue bird flashcard) "Show me the blue bird." (Students point to the flashcard). "What color is it?" (Students say "Blue!").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in Our World."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated mini-story book or large flashcards with a short sentence per card, featuring a simple narrative using target vocabulary and grammar. The genre is a simple narrative, the register is informal and child-friendly, and the format is a picture story. The story will include elements like: "It is an orange cat. Look! The cat can see a blue bird. The blue bird can fly. It sees a red flower. Many green leaves grow on the tall tree. That is a purple house."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the illustrated mini-story, "The Colorful World." The teacher shows each picture card, pointing to the simple words as they read aloud. For example, pointing to the picture of the cat, then the word "cat," then the word "orange."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud clearly, pausing at each picture and sentence, using gestures and intonation to convey meaning (e.g., miming a bird flying).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and look at the pictures. The teacher encourages students to point to the objects and colors mentioned as they hear them (e.g., "Point to the blue bird!").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing colors and objects: Identify familiar objects by color (e.g., "blue bird" or "red flower"). Describing with colors: Use simple color phrases in sentences (e.g., "That is a red bird.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist questions):</b> After reading the story, the teacher asks very simple comprehension questions, prompting students to point to pictures.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is the cat?" (Students point to the orange cat picture.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a blue bird and a red flower picture) "Is the bird blue or red?" (Students point to the blue bird.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can the bird fly?" (Students show "yes" or "no" with a gesture, then point to the flying bird picture.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Color + noun (e.g., "blue bird"), Present simple (e.g., "It is an orange cat.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky, blue, purple, orange, green, red, tall, small.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Identifying initial sounds of letters of the alphabet in common objects and things (e.g., /f/ in "fish," /b/ in "bird," /k/ in "cat").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Picture-Word Matching):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures from the story (e.g., an orange cat, a blue bird, a red flower) on one side and the corresponding simple words ("cat," "bird," "flower") in a mixed order on the other side. Students draw a line to match each picture to its correct word. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-Controlled Practice - Color-Object Phrase Reading):</b> The teacher provides sentence strips with phrases like "It is a ______ cat." and offers two word cards (e.g., "orange," "green"). Students read the sentence and insert the correct color word to match a picture shown by the teacher (e.g., a picture of an orange cat). The teacher also shows pictures and asks, "What color is this leaf?" and students identify the color and read the word "green" on a card. This reinforces "color + noun" structure and word recognition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Sound and Word Recognition Check):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of mixed flashcards: some with pictures (e.g., a fish, a bird, a cat) and some with single words ("fish," "bird," "cat"). Student A shows a picture (e.g., a picture of a cat) and says the initial sound /k/. Student B finds and reads the word "cat" and shows it. They then swap roles. This activity specifically targets phonemic awareness and reading simple words.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in Our World."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Colorful Picture Story Reading."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides each student with a small booklet containing 3-4 blank pages and a set of picture stickers (e.g., orange cat, blue bird, green tree, red flower) and corresponding word stickers ("cat," "bird," "tree," "flower").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to stick one picture on each page and then find and stick the correct simple word under each picture. For example, on one page, they stick the "orange cat" picture and then the word "cat" sticker below it.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once completed, students work in small groups (2-3 students). Each student "reads" their mini-book to their group by pointing to the picture and then the word, identifying it (e.g., pointing to "cat" and saying "cat"). They also try to identify the color if possible (e.g., "orange cat").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher observes, offering gentle correction and encouraging students to identify the words they have placed. The focus is on the act of reading (identifying) the words and pictures they have arranged.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students actively engage with "Colors in Our World" by creating and "reading" their own simple "It's an Orange Cat" themed picture books, demonstrating their ability to identify key objects and their associated words.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the "My Colorful Picture Story Reading" task to note which students can identify simple words and match them to pictures. Additionally, a short individual assessment task is administered.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a worksheet. Part 1: It shows 3-4 pictures from the story (e.g., a blue bird, a green leaf, an orange cat) and a list of 3-4 simple words ("bird," "leaf," "cat"). Students draw a line from each picture to its correct word. Part 2: Students see a sequence of 2-3 pictures illustrating key events from the story (e.g., orange cat, blue bird, red flower). The teacher reads a simple sentence (e.g., "The cat is orange.") and students circle the picture that matches what was heard/read.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher checklist):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify pictures that show what happened in a simple story (e.g., circling the blue bird when "blue bird" is heard/read).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify simple words for very common symbols (e.g., matching "cat" to its picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify initial sounds of letters of the alphabet when supported with pictures (e.g., recognizing /k/ in "cat" during guided practice).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a paper with two statements and a happy face / sad face. They circle the face that best describes how they feel.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can match the word 'cat' to the picture of a cat." 😊 🙁</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I know what color the cat is in the story." 😊 🙁</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher asks the students to briefly reflect on their learning:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you read today?" (Students might point to words or pictures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite color word to read?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What reading activities worked well for my 1st-grade students? Did they successfully match pictures to words and identify initial sounds?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with word recognition or phonemic awareness? (e.g., more visual cues, slower pacing, one-on-one support).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> Today, students focused on reading and identifying words and pictures. In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), they will use these identified words and concepts to actively speak and interact, describing the colors and objects they have now learned to recognize in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>